in guatemala's national palace  u.s vice president kamala harris issued  a blunt message  to central american migrants looking to  travel  to the united states i want to be clear  to folks in this region who are thinking  about making  that dangerous trek to the united states  mexico border  do not come do not come  harris was tasked in march by president  joe biden  to help stem the record flow of migrants  that have surged along the southern  border of the united states and mexico  since he took office a majority from  guatemala  well standing next to harris in remarks  to the press  guatemalan president alejandro giamette  said the solution  is increased economic opportunity so  migrants will choose to stay  in their homeland people leave due to a  lack of opportunities  municipalities that are areas of greater  migration  coincide with the areas where poverty is  greater  so we need to fight poverty but  corruption  is another challenge the united states  has given guatemala more than 1.6  billion in aid over the past decade  to fight economic instability and  violence  but residents of this struggling  neighborhood in guatemala city  say that money rarely reaches those that  need it  guatemala is in an economic crisis the  country doesn't produce a lot of jobs so  the youth turn to violence  the biden administration has already  committed to sending  310 million to the region it's part of a  four billion  four-year plan to improve the economy in  central america  still other residents are skeptical  harris's diplomatic visit  will result in any real change  i feel it would be good if she came to  visit us to help the kids to help my  neighbors  we need the help here it's criticism  harris is  also facing back in the united states  republicans argue  harris needs to visit the u.s mexico  border to see the problem of migration  first hand but harris insists that's not  necessary i came here to be here on the  ground  to speak with the leader of this nation  around what we can do  in a way that is significant is  tangible and has real results and i will  continue to be focused  on that kind of work as opposed to grand  gestures  the surge of migrants from central  america to  the united states is a problem that has  been years in the making and will not be  solved with just one meeting  that's why the u.s vice president on  tuesday will also be meeting with the  president  of mexico as the leaders continue to  collaborate  and find a solution kimberly hellcat al  jazeera guatemala city
 

